  "content": {
    "title": "Michael B. Jordan Wins the Best Actor Oscar for ‘Sinners’ - The New York Times",
    "text": "AdvertisementSKIP ADVERTISEMENT See more of our coverage in your search results.Encuentra más de nuestra cobertura en los resultados de búsqueda. Add The New York Times on GoogleAgrega The New York Times en Google Michael B. Jordan won his first Oscar on Sunday, for playing the twins Smoke and Stack in “Sinners,” Ryan Coogler’s blockbuster vampire allegory about race in America. It was also Jordan’s first Oscar nomination.Jordan triumphed over Timothée Chalamet for “Marty Supreme,” Ethan Hawke for “Blue Moon,” Wagner Moura for “The Secret Agent” and Leonardo DiCaprio for “One Battle After Another.” The win makes him the sixth Black man ever to prevail in the category after Will Smith in 2022, Forest Whitaker in 2007, Jamie Foxx in 2005, Denzel Washington in 2002 and Sidney Poitier in 1964.Jordan, 39, listed all those men in his acceptance speech, along with Halle Berry, who won the best actress Oscar in 2002 for her performance in “Monster’s Ball,” making her the first and only Black woman to win in the category.Subscribe to The Times to read as many articles as you like.Maya Salam is an editor and reporter, focusing primarily on pop culture across genres.See more on: Academy Awards (Oscars)Share full articleRelated ContentAdvertisementSKIP ADVERTISEMENT Enjoy unlimited access to all of The Times. See subscription options Go",
